Bassam Tariq revealed in a new interview with The New Arab that the biggest lesson he learned as the original director of Marvel's failed " Blade " movie is "life&#x2019;s too short to make shit films."

"It was funny and strange to start casting and building without a proper script," Tariq said abot his short-lived Marvel career. "You&#x2019;re sitting there and explaining to actors what their role will be, and they just have to believe you. That&#x2019;s also very dangerous because if the text comes and it doesn&#x2019;t add up to what you were saying, your credibility gets completely shot. All I have is my integrity, and I&#x2019;ve been thinking about that a lot." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Marrakech's Atlas Workshops Takes Victory Lap as Industry Event Celebrates Cannes, Venice Triumphs Claes Bang, Emma Mackey, Vicky Krieps and More Join Marion Cotillard in Marrakech Jury

"If you&#x2019;re the bearer of bad news to your actors, but you&#x2019;re not the one making any of these decisions &#x2014; and it&#x2019;s actually coming from a corporate entity &#x2014; that&#x2019;s not a great way to be a director," the director continued. "I know some people can take that and run with it, but I learned I was losing credibility with my actors, and it wasn&#x2019;t a healthy way to operate as a filmmaker. Life&#x2019;s too short to make shit films, so that was my big lesson: I have to have full control over my script. If we disagree, it&#x2019;s between them and me. It&#x2019;s quite sad because we had such high hopes for what we wanted to build."

Marvel originally announced &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; at Comic-Con in July 2019 with Oscar winner Mahershala Ali set to star in the title role. Tariq (who went on to direct Ali in &#x201C;Your Mother Your Mother Your Mother&#x201D;) was the first director to join the movie, but he exited in September 2022 roughly two months before it was set to begin shooting. Yann Demange stepped in as the movie&#x2019;s director after Tariq&#x2019;s exit, but Demange ended up dropping out in June 2024. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Variety reported in November 2023 that &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; had gone through at least five different writers while in development, from &#x201C;Watchmen&#x201D; scribe Stacy Osei-Kuffour to Michael Starrbury. The movie was ultimately scrapped and never went into production.

Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige told the &#x201C;Happy Sad Confused&#x201D; podcast over the summer that he was &#x201C;feeling like a gigantic loser and failure&#x201D; for failing to get Ali&#x2019;s &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; made, to which Ali agreed with during a later interview with GQ magazine.

&#x201C;That&#x2019;s a Kevin Feige question,&#x201D; Ali said when asked why Marvel never got to the point of putting &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; into production. &#x201C;They had, as you said, a two-time Oscar-winning actor under contract and you got billions of dollars. That&#x2019;s a question for them. I don&#x2019;t mean anything personal to you, but I don&#x2019;t know why it didn&#x2019;t. To this day, I don&#x2019;t know why it didn&#x2019;t happen. I just know that it was&#x2014;Kevin, he said it was the biggest failure of his career and I agree.&#x201D;

Amid the film&#x2019;s development struggles, Ali&#x2019;s interest in playing Blade never wavered. He told Variety on the &#x201C;Jurassic World Rebirth&#x201D; red carpet in 2015: &#x201C;I&#x2019;m ready. Let them know I&#x2019;m ready.&#x201D; In an interview with GQ earlier this year, Ali called out Marvel point blank by saying: &#x201C;You had me under contract, they have billions of dollars, if they wanted to do the movie, we would&#x2019;ve done the movie. So we&#x2019;re not doing the movie.&#x201D;

Feige explained in July 2025 how the prolonged delays on &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; were a symptom of Marvel&#x2019;s &#x201C;over-expansion&#x201D; into Disney+ streaming content where &#x201C;for the very first time ever, quantity trumped quality.&#x201D; When Marvel began to reevaluate its development process, the &#x201C;Blade&#x201D; movie was one of the projects that stalled.

Fresh off receiving the Faten Hamama Excellence Award at the 47th Cairo Intl. Film Festival, Egyptian actor Asser Yassin is looking ahead. For the star, whose career spans two decades across independent cinema, commercial films and television, the recognition comes at a moment of renewed ambition and a desire to continue pushing himself creatively.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s a sort of assurance,&#x201D; Yassin tells Variety . &#x201C;Any artist has this kind of hesitation sometimes. Did I do it right? Am I doing it right? What&#x2019;s next?&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Marrakech Film Festival Grows Its Awards-Season Clout With 'Artificial,' 'The Debut,' 'Fjord' and Other Oscar Hopefuls Mark Ruffalo, Tilda Swinton, Michael Moore, Barry Jenkins and More Rally to the Defense of Iranian Filmmaker Jafar Panahi

Rather than viewing the honor as a culmination of his career, Yassin sees it as inspiration to continue evolving. &#x201C;Doubt is always there. Hesitation is always there,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;Faith is the light. And this is a proof of that light.&#x201D;

That ambition is already taking shape in his latest project, &#x201C;Asal Ahmar&#x201D; (&#x201C;Red Honey&#x201D;), a psychological thriller adapted from a novel by Egyptian writer Ibrahim Eissa. The series reunites Yassin with Egyptian-Tunisian actress Hend Sabry and marks his first collaboration with director Mariam Ahmadi, with whom he says he has long wanted to work.

Drawn to the intensity of the source material, Yassin describes a role with &#x201C;a very dark side&#x201D; that explores themes of motherhood, delving into his character&#x2019;s relationship with his mother. &#x201C;I always like, every now and then, to indulge in an intense character,&#x201D; he explains.

For the actor, the role also represents an opportunity to apply lessons learned over the course of his career, particularly when it comes to separating himself from emotionally demanding performances. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;I love the fact that I&#x2019;m disconnecting myself completely and shielding myself completely from the character when I just finished shooting,&#x201D; he notes. &#x201C;This is something I wasn&#x2019;t able to do years ago.&#x201D;

That willingness to challenge himself has defined Yassin&#x2019;s career, from character-driven dramas such as &#x201C;Messages From the Sea&#x201D; and &#x201C;Rags and Tatters&#x201D; to commercial productions and television.

&#x201C;One of the major things that I tried to maintain growing up as an actor was not to embrace the norm,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;The norm is that you have to be a box-office movie. I think this is very limiting. My career was shaped upon variation, on taking risks, on exploring, on adventuring. I&#x2019;m not bound by any previous choices that shaped my career.&#x201D;

His attitude toward collaboration has also changed. Earlier in his career, Yassin preferred to work with a director only once, eager to explore as many creative partnerships as possible. Today, he finds himself drawn back to filmmakers with whom he worked years ago.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s lovely when you meet a director that you&#x2019;ve worked with before,&#x201D; he reflects. &#x201C;Especially that we worked together 20 years ago, or 15 years ago, or 10 years ago. Now there&#x2019;s a new version of him or her and of me that are meeting again to re-explore whatever is happening in the present.&#x201D;

That appreciation for artistic growth extends to the mentors who helped shape his early career, particularly veteran Egyptian filmmaker Khairy Beshara. Having studied mechanical engineering before pursuing acting, Yassin recalls the importance of having someone guide him beyond the initial ambition of simply being seen on screen.

Beshara, he says, encouraged him to approach acting not simply as a profession, but as an extension of his experiences and of his understanding of life. &#x201C;Being a human being first. The love for life. The love for the passion,&#x201D; Yassin recalls. Through conversations, recommended films and readings, Beshara taught him that good acting was not about commanding attention, but about being truthful on screen.

&#x201C;The actor shouldn&#x2019;t be the one who&#x2019;s very loud, trying to prove himself on screen,&#x201D; Yassin remembers Beshara telling him. &#x201C;The actor should be really truthful to what he&#x2019;s doing. He really feels what he&#x2019;s doing and the eyes would show that, so focus on your eyes.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The experience has inspired him to consider his own responsibility toward emerging talent. &#x201C;I truly believe in mentors and I want to be a mentor for someone,&#x201D; he adds, explaining that he makes time for young actors who approach him for advice.

Looking toward the next chapter of his career, Yassin is interested in a return to the stage and is increasingly open to developing projects of his own. &#x201C;Maybe it&#x2019;s time for me to start thinking in that void again and produce something myself, be it theater, a film, or something I write.&#x201D;

Beyond exploring new creative paths, Yassin is even more focused on the kind of legacy he will leave behind through his work. &#x201C;I want to be in movies that live,&#x201D; he emphasizes, arguing that commercial success and lasting appeal need not be mutually exclusive.

Yassin is equally optimistic about the future of Egyptian cinema, despite rising production costs and the challenges facing independent filmmaking. He believes greater collaboration within the industry could help address those obstacles, while younger audiences exposed to films from around the world are pushing filmmakers to explore new directions.

&#x201C;The audience is telling us they need new, different movies, not just commercial box-office movies. They need something new,&#x201D; he argues. &#x201C;I think there is a new wave that is shaping up. I can see it and I&#x2019;m very excited about it.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Artificial&#x201D; screenwriter Simon Rich has joined the short film &#x201C;Houston, We Have a Crush&#x201D; as executive producer.

Directed by Omer Ben-Shachar and co-written with New Yorker humorist Jennifer Kim, the live-action short follows Ditto, a lonely alien who discovers a phone accidentally left behind by an astronaut. As he explores its contents, Ditto develops an all-consuming crush on the phone&#x2019;s owner. Related Stories ‘Green Room’ Director Remembers Patrick Stewart as a ‘F—ing Sweetheart’ and Credits Him for Rescuing the Film: He ‘Swooped Down’ ‘Into Our Cruddy Ass Movie and Saved Us’ Abdul El-Sayed Mocks Trump and Ted Cruz's Cruel Rhetoric on 'The View,' Jokes He Wants 'Jihadist Leprechaun' Printed on a Hat

The New Yorker has acquired the sci-fi project for an upcoming premiere on its digital platforms, including YouTube. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Rich is a frequent contributor to The New Yorker. His other credits include &#x201C;Saturday Night Live,&#x201D; &#x201C;Man Seeking Woman,&#x201D; &#x201C;Inside Out&#x201D; and &#x201C;Miracle Workers.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I'm thrilled to present this hilarious and heartbreaking short directed by the brilliant Omer Ben-Shachar,&#x201D; Rich said in a statement on Friday. &#x201C;Co-written by Ben-Shachar and Jen Kim, &#x2018;Houston, We Have a Crush&#x2019; is a brutally relatable story of loneliness and unrequited love that speaks to the freakish alien bird in all of us. I'm so glad a movie this strange and this beautiful exists and I can't wait to see what Omer and Jen create next.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Houston, We Have a Crush&#x201D; has screened at more than 40 film festivals worldwide, including Palm Springs International ShortFest, NewFest, Guadalajara, Austin, Urbanworld, Indy Shorts, HollyShorts and SXSW Sydney, where it was nominated for best narrative short. Its accolades include the Art Directors Guild (ADG, IATSE Local 800) Production Design Award at the GuadaLAjara Film Festival, the Jury Award for best fanomenon short at the Leeds International Film Festival and best international short film at the 43rd Sulmona Film Festival. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Jen and I thought it would be funny if an astronaut accidentally left his phone in space, and an alien found it and fell in love with him based on what was inside,&#x201D; said Ben-Shachar. &#x201C;But the idea came from something very personal. We&#x2019;ve both experienced that feeling of projecting our fantasies onto someone we barely know, only to discover that the person we&#x2019;ve imagined doesn&#x2019;t quite exist. We wanted to explore that very human experience through the eyes of an alien who, in many ways, feels just like us.&#x201D;

The film stars Sam Humphrey as Ditto and Ben Rigby as the astronaut. Jason Eisner produced, with Bara Kim coproducing. In addition to Rich, executive producers include Hollywood Ventures Group, Claudio Olachea and Kim.

Ben-Shachar previously directed &#x201C;Tree #3,&#x201D; which won a Student Academy Award and received a Student Emmy nomination. His short &#x201C;Single Residence Occupancy&#x201D; won Imagine Entertainment&#x2019;s &#x201C;From Minutes to Movies ShortFest&#x201D; and the Atlanta Film Festival&#x2019;s Jury Award for Best Narrative Short. His films have screened at more than 100 festivals internationally, with previous work featured on Short of the Week and Vimeo Staff Picks.

The short is currently being developed as a feature film.

Tilda Swinton and Emma Mackey , " Narnia 's" White Witches of past and present, were both on hand at the Marrakech Film Festival opening ceremony on Friday night.

Swinton played the character in the original "Chronicles of Narnia" film trilogy, whereas Mackey &#x2014; who is serving on the festival's jury &#x2014; takes on the role in Greta Gerwig's upcoming "The Magician's Nephew" movie at Netflix. Related Stories ‘Green Room’ Director Remembers Patrick Stewart as a ‘F—ing Sweetheart’ and Credits Him for Rescuing the Film: He ‘Swooped Down’ ‘Into Our Cruddy Ass Movie and Saved Us’ Abdul El-Sayed Mocks Trump and Ted Cruz's Cruel Rhetoric on 'The View,' Jokes He Wants 'Jihadist Leprechaun' Printed on a Hat

Speaking to Variety on the red carpet, Swinton &#x2014; who served as Marrakech jury president in 2019 &#x2014; said she hasn&#x2019;t yet met Mackey, but hopes to say hello this weekend. &#x201C;I don't know her … I&#x2019;ll say, 'I don&#x2019;t know you, but nice to meet you!'" she said. "I'm excited for her." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Mackey, on the other hand, said they have a group dinner planned for Saturday night, where they&#x2019;ll officially meet for the first time. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s thrilling, my absolute dream," she said.

Does Mackey plan to ask Swinton for any advice? &#x201C;I mean, what would I… She's the queen. I wouldn&#x2019;t even know how to breathe around her, I don&#x2019;t think. So step by step.&#x201D;

As for her role on the jury, Mackey said: "I'm just so happy to be here."

"I love that it&#x2019;s a festival that honors first and second time filmmakers. I think it&#x2019;s very smart," she continued. "You know how hard it is to make a movie and get them financed and how miraculous it is to get everything aligned, so I&#x2019;m very hopeful and I&#x2019;m happy to be a part of that.&#x201D;

This year's edition of Marrakech Film Festival is presided over by Marion Cotillard, with jurors joining Mackey including "Phantom Thread" star Vicky Krieps, Danish actor Claes Bang, French actor-director Gilles Lellouche, "The Voice of Hind Rajab" helmer Kaouther Ben Hania, "All of Us Strangers" filmmaker Andrew Haigh, Franco-Senegalese helmer Ramata-Toulaye Sy and Moroccan director-producer Sa&#xEF;d Hamich Benlarbi. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The Marrakech Film Festival runs through Nov. 28.

Oscar-winning documentarian Laura Poitras is acutely aware of the importance of the weight of words when describing issues of great sensitivity. Her work has dealt with Edward Snowden&#x2019;s NSA whistleblowing, the Sackler family&#x2019;s role in the opioid crisis, and Iraq under U.S. occupation, among other burning sociopolitical issues of the 21st century. With &#x201C; They&#x2019;re Here ,&#x201D; the lauded director turns her lens on the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories ‘Green Room’ Director Remembers Patrick Stewart as a ‘F—ing Sweetheart’ and Credits Him for Rescuing the Film: He ‘Swooped Down’ ‘Into Our Cruddy Ass Movie and Saved Us’ ‘Avengers: Doomsday’ Including the Original X-Men ‘Just Felt Right,’ Says Kevin Feige: It's a 'Fun Way to Culminate' 25 Years

Co-directed with Rachel Lauren Muller, &#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; combines footage captured by the filmmakers with material provided by activists on the ground in Minneapolis during violent ICE raids in the aftermath of the killing of Alex Pretti and Ren&#xE9;e Good. In the short film, images of a victim barricading himself in the trunk of a car to get to work give way to a volunteer resistance organization tracking ICE raids through a citizen-fed counter-surveillance software system. In the film, words like "war zone" are used to describe what it felt like to be in Minneapolis in January of this year.

Speaking with Variety out of IDFA , Poitras says language has been &#x201C;really important in all of my work.&#x201D; &#x201C;We have to reject the euphemisms of the government to describe things. This was a paramilitary occupation of an American city that created an effective war zone.&#x201D;

&#x201C;My co-director, Rachel Lauren Mueller, lives in the neighborhood that we filmed, so she can talk about the fact that there are helicopters hovering, tanks and people being executed in broad daylight,&#x201D; she adds. &#x201C;We need to use the word execution, use the word abduction, kidnapping, occupation, paramilitary force. Because those are the actual English words that describe what&#x2019;s happening. When you use words like deportation, you&#x2019;re capitulating to the government's narrative, which I don't think we should ever do.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Poitras was also interested in the idea of citizens using their phones and available technology at hand to counter-surveil ICE, which she believes to be a &#x201C;very effective form of resistance.&#x201D; &#x201C;State powers need to know that they're being watched, that they can't do this violence in secrecy with total impunity. There will be accountability. There has to be accountability.&#x201D; Citizens, the filmmaker says, know &#x201C;that this is a paramilitary occupation of the United States happening with absolutely no accountability,&#x201D; and their footage is a way to &#x201C;have a record that can be used both as a deterrent and as evidence and to tell the truth.&#x201D;

&#x201C;As a political statement, it's important to say that the master's tools will never dismantle the master's house,&#x201D; she adds. &#x201C;There's only so much we can do. But there is something that's powerful about documenting and counter-surveilling what the state is doing and making sure that they know that they're also being watched and make them nervous about that.&#x201D;

&#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; is permeated by a sustained sense of dread, borrowing from genre to amplify both the loud, anxiety-inducing frenzy of the raids and the dystopian images of lined-up people slowly marched into freight planes from the same airport where thousands of Americans jet off to holidays. The visual language was inspired by films like Alfonso Cuar&#xF3;n&#x2019;s &#x201C;Children of Men&#x201D; and RaMell Ross&#x2019; &#x201C;Nickel Boys.&#x201D;

&#x201C;We very much wanted to use the tools of cinema to capture the sense of horror that was unfolding and to create that uncanny distance of a reality that is unfolding as we speak,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;We're seeing so much violence, and we don&#x2019;t want to repeat it. How can we denormalize it and put the audience in a perspective of both fear and terror, but also a critical distance where you understand how profoundly abnormal what&#x2019;s happening in the United States is right now?&#x201D;

When asked if witnessing the citizen-led efforts in Minneapolis fed a sense of hope, Poitras says she doesn&#x2019;t know if &#x201C;hope is the right word given the state of the world,&#x201D; but that she believes resistance &#x201C;does have an impact.&#x201D;

Addressing the Democrats' recent success in the U.S. midterm elections, she note, &#x201C;These victories of the progressive movement in the U.S. didn&#x2019;t happen overnight because of some charismatic people. Yes, of course, there are great candidates, but it was years and decades of work laying the foundation without visible results and with many defeats. When you see these successes, you realize, ok, this is a moment. There are tipping points and tables are turned and nobody can surrender our democracies.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

On top of presenting &#x201C;They&#x2019;re Here&#x201D; at IDFA and being on the festival jury, Poitras hosted an awards screening and Q&A for Lance Oppenheim and Nathan Fielder&#x2019;s doc-phenomenon &#x201C;You Can See Everything&#x201D; while on the ground at the Dutch festival.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s totally, 100% a documentary,&#x201D; she says, quickly rebuffing any idea that the majorly successful film could be anything but. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s dealing with ethical questions of documentary and lifting the fourth wall. Plus, it&#x2019;s fantastic drama. There&#x2019;s this chess match, this psychological thriller between Nathan Fielder, Elizabeth Holmes and Billy Evans that is just fascinating.&#x201D;

